WEBVTT

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.510
This is Ed Torgerson at the Indianapolis Speedway.

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:22.890
That's where 500 miles on the speedway are the equal of 50,000 miles on the highway.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:36.390
Indianapolis Motor Speedway, proving ground of progress for the automobile of tomorrow.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:46.610
May 30th, 1946, and 185,000 spectators from far and near gather to witness a thrilling

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:52.870
contest featuring the skill of daring drivers, the genius of engineers and designers, and

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:54.670
the teamwork of the crews in the pit.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:58.860
The 30th running of the Indianapolis 500-mile race.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.790
Some, a Roman holiday of thrills and chills.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:07.730
To those who know, another outstanding chapter in the history of an event

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:11.540
which has contributed so much to the development of the modern automobile.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:17.930
You will find in every modern automobile abundant evidence of the improvements

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:22.210
and new designs which were developed and tested in this crucible of speed.

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:28.960
High compression engines, balloon tires, high octane fuel.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.570
wheel brakes, independent wheel suspension, even the rear view mirror. Such vital developments

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:43.910
as these came straight from the 500 mile race to your car. The garage at the very heart of

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:49.750
the Speedway infield is a man known to every Indianapolis driver. He is Firestone's Johnny

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:56.090
Moore. They depend on him for the tires that safeguard their lives during the 500 mile grind.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.170
Johnny, how's everything?

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:08.530
Fine, Carl. I'm glad to see you. What brings you down from the press stand?

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.660
Well, the Times Syndicate wants me to do a series of articles on the history of the race.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:13.930
I need help.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:18.360
They want me to write up the automotive advancements that have developed on the Speedway.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:22.450
Well, you've come to the right place. There's two men over there that can help you a lot.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.280
The one on the right is the new president of the Speedway.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:26.240
Wilbur Shaw.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:27.880
I recognize him.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:29.140
Who's the other one?

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:29.980
Ray Haroon.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.340
the first Indianapolis winner that's right come on I'll introduce you to them

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:39.950
well Bert I want you to meet Carl Reed of the Times Syndicate he's an old friend of mine

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:45.360
pleasure to meet you mr. Reed how are you mr. fine thank you Carl's writing a series of stories

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.320
on the history of the race and he's looking for some information if it's history you're

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:54.120
interested in here's a gentleman you should meet mr. Ray Haroon winner of the first 500 mile race

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:59.970
in 1911 how do you do sir how do you do glad to help you mr. Reed remember I

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.480
go back 35 years I'm really an old-timer so much the better you know

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.960
lots of people have the idea that the Indianapolis race is nothing more than

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:13.150
a sporting event I'd like to tell them how much the race has contributed to the

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:18.410
automotive industry should be a great story and well worth telling the current

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:22.890
which he won the first 500 mile race is just outside and it has a gadget on I'm

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:26.440
sure you'd be interested in seeing it fits right in doesn't it right well

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:27.100
let's go see it

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.510
Don't expect to see anything very glamorous.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.700
She was a beautiful automobile in her day.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.160
Still beautiful to me.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:42.120
There she is.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:44.560
Winter, 1911.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:47.500
Marmon Wasp.

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:49.890
And it took a lot of courage to drive this baby.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:55.480
This car is historic for several reasons, Mr. Reed.

00:03:57.290 --> 00:03:59.150
We designed this car to carry one man.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:03.400
I figured I could save weight and get more speed by riding alone.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:10.180
The job of the mechanic in those days was to watch behind and notify the driver when other cars were trying to pass.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:16.780
They claimed that it would be dangerous for me to ride alone, so I rigged up this little gadget.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:20.180
This, Mr. Reed, is the first rear-vision mirror.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:25.470
It must have been a great race, Ray.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:26.880
I'd like to have seen it.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:29.960
It was a great race, Wilbur, and I don't think...

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.760
anybody that saw it will ever forget it 40 speed kings of two continents lined up on the new brick

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:43.740
track on the morning of may 30th 1911 for the first running of the indianapolis 500 mile race

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:49.890
once around the track in formation at 60 miles an hour then the battle began as they crossed the

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:54.940
starting line johnny aiken and his number four national took the lead for about three laps

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:59.480
and the boys began to get into trouble as the fast pace caused minor accidents

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:06.630
Spencer Wishart in the Mercedes number 11 took over the lead into the 12th lap and then David

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:12.010
Bruce Brown's number 28 Fiat went out in front to lead almost every lap up to the halfway mark

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:24.150
80 laps Ray Haroon in number 32 was still holding third place behind Bruce Brown and Ralph De Palma

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:36.810
In the 87th lap, Joe Jaegersberger came down the straightaway in his case number eight.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.060
His steering gear broke and his mechanic fell out onto the track.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:45.360
To avoid hitting the mechanic, Harry Knight threw his waistcoat into a skid

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.800
and crashed into car number 35 in the pits.

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:53.050
Knight's car then turned a loop in the air and landed on Eddie Hearn's Fiat number 18.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.400
All four of the cars involved were out of business for the rest of the race.

00:05:59.180 --> 00:05:59.980
Because of...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.880
pit work, Bruce Brown lost the lead and Haroon went out in front at 250 miles with Ralph

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:12.280
Mulford pushing hard at second position. At the end of six hours and 42 minutes, Haroon

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:17.040
was mighty glad to be coming down the straightaway with a checkered flag. His average for the

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.980
500 miles was just under 75 miles an hour.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:29.520
75 miles an hour isn't very fast today, but it seemed plenty fast to me back in 1911.

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:34.070
No matter how fast you go, that's the longest 500 miles on earth.

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:35.480
You ought to know.

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:38.700
As a third-time winner, Mr. Shaw, I'd say you qualified as an expert.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:40.540
It must have been a tough grind.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:42.620
It's still a tough grind, and no question.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:44.850
Hello, Ralph.

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:46.440
Hello, Johnny.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.580
Here's another Indianapolis veteran you don't want to miss.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:54.680
The only man who ever tried to win by pushing his car across the finish line.

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:56.760
That would be Ralph De Palma.

00:06:56.860 --> 00:06:58.100
Ralph, it's nice to see you.

00:06:58.470 --> 00:06:59.050
See you indeed.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:06.420
Well, Roy, what a pleasure. Carl Reid, Ralph De Palma. Well, Mr. Reid, how are you? How do you do, Mr. De Palma?

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:12.000
Ralph, tell him about your glorious failure of 1912, will you? Glorious failure.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:22.850
Well, Mr. Reid, I tried very hard to win. You know, I was leading by 22 and a half miles, about one and a half laps from the finish.

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:29.280
The car broke down at the north end, turned at the end of the stretch at Indianapolis.

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:34.400
I had one idea in mind, and that was to win the race.

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:37.770
My mechanic and myself got out and pushed the car to the table.

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:42.280
They don't pay off on that. The car must cross the line under its own power.

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:44.800
Ralph didn't always have tough luck.

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:48.020
During 27 years of racing, he won plenty of races.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:49.720
It takes more than luck to win a race.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:50.920
Righto. Thank you, Carl.

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:55.590
Carl is doing a series of articles on automotive advancements

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:57.910
that have been developed here in the race.

00:07:58.520 --> 00:07:59.900
Do you remember anything special?

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.340
Roth that was developed in the 1913 race yes that was the air Terry Tesla came

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:08.930
here with an Assata Froschini with four-wheel brakes the first one seen in

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:13.470
this country you won the 1915 race did you mr. De Palma yes I did just barely

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:17.660
got across the line with a broken piston I remember that finish Dario

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:22.240
Resta was right behind you and believe me we had our fingers crossed the track

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:28.370
closed down during the first war yes they made a flying field out of it they

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:29.870
started racing again in 19

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:40.340
19 he called it the Liberty sweepstakes for the first time we had multi-cylinder racing engine French Ballot cars had straight eight engines and

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:43.160
You had a twin six-packer didn't you Ralph?

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:53.670
You know Carl in the early days the greatest problem around the speedway was tires blowouts tire changes caused more delays than

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:59.760
Mechanical failures it was about this time that Harvey s Firestone became interested in the race

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:07.260
Yes, he told our engineers if they could build a tire to withstand the torture of 500 miles on the speedway,

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:11.490
they would be building the safest tire for the family car that we had ever seen.

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:17.660
He knew that 500 miles on the speedway is equal to 50,000 miles on the highway.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:21.640
Our development department went to work on the assignment,

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:26.650
and in 1920, Gaston Chevrolet won the race on Firestone tires.

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:29.960
And Chevrolet won with our...

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.530
of tire change the first time on the speedway. Mr. Firestone's judgment was well rewarded,

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:42.910
and from that time on, every race at Indianapolis has been won on Firestone tires. Passenger

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:49.090
guard tires increased in safety and mileage as a direct result. As a matter of fact, a lot of

00:09:49.090 --> 00:09:54.730
automotive advancements developed out of the 1920 race. The first American car with four-wheel

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:59.900
brakes and a straight-eight engine appeared on the track, and the 1920 race also featured

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:07.980
The first high-efficiency battery ignition Wilbur knows his stuff girl comes naturally

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:14.190
I was raised here in Indianapolis and my one ambition was to be a racing driver

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:19.160
I practically haunted this place around here until I was large enough to sit behind the steering wheel

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:24.440
What's the next big development? Oh, I believe the supercharger was wouldn't you say so Wilbur?

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:29.740
Oh, I think so too a Duesenberg car with a centrifugal supercharger won the 1920

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:37.550
race. That made possible the turbo supercharger of today. And it also made possible the performance

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:43.830
of our big bombers and fighter planes in World War II. 1924 was the first year that

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:49.160
ethyl gasoline was used in the Indianapolis race. That was an important development. Speaking

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:54.950
of contributions, how about Pete De Palo's win in 1925? Remember Wilbur? He was the first

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:59.870
driver to average over 100 miles an hour. You can give balloon tires credit for that, Mr. Reed.

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:03.650
The tires were designed, developed, and built by Firestone.

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:08.030
It's one of the best examples I know of a product that was proved on the speedway.

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:11.720
Balloon tires certainly made a big difference in passenger cars.

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:13.760
All the difference in the world.

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:17.150
They met entirely new standards of speed and safety.

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:20.520
I can remember how they used to demonstrate balloon tires.

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:25.510
You know, with high-pressure tires, riding on rough roads was pretty rugged.

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:29.560
Every bump was tough on the car, and tougher is still on the rider.

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:34.290
Yet, with balloon tires, the difference was, well, the difference between day and night.

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:38.570
The bumps were ironed out, the passengers rode in real comfort.

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:42.240
I'd almost forgotten about the old high-pressure tire.

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:48.940
I don't suppose many people realize that the balloon tires were first used in the 1925 Indianapolis race.

00:11:49.700 --> 00:11:54.520
You're right, Wilbur. Front-wheel-drive cars were also raced the first time in 1925.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:56.800
That's the I introduced to drops on the rims.

00:11:57.940 --> 00:11:58.460
1925.

00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:07.270
important here in my story I'd say. In the 1930 race, hydraulic shock absorbers came into general use.

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:13.440
They were adopted for the family automobiles immediately. Even more important, the rules barred

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:19.300
superchargers. That meant racing engineers had to redesign engines to increase horsepower and speed.

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:27.260
It was the start of our modern high-compression engine. 1934 brought out the first racing car

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:29.970
with four-wheel drive. There was another...

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:37.030
development in 1934. The rules limited each car to 45 gallons of gasoline and

00:12:37.030 --> 00:12:42.830
engines had to be redesigned for economy. Bill Cummins won the race that year. I

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:46.930
think he had about three gallons of gasoline left in the tank. Here again the

00:12:46.930 --> 00:12:51.060
things we tested at the Speedway were used in the passenger cars the public

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:54.930
bought in the years that followed. They kept lowering the gasoline allowance every

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:59.110
year after that didn't they? For a while anyway. Yes, they got down to the 37 and a

00:12:59.110 --> 00:12:59.970
half gallons in 19-

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:07.040
1930s they were trying to hold down the speed of the cars and at the same time encouraged design for a greater fuel economy

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:14.840
But each year the designers and the drivers outguessed the experts and the speeds went up and up you won the race in

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:21.500
1937 39 and 40 didn't you mr. Shaw? Yes in the 1939 race

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:26.320
I used a new type of lightweight alloy rim and in 1940

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:29.910
Firestone added an important improvement by building a wide

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.280
base rim that meant greater stability for the car more positive steering

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:40.880
control and greater safety on the curves Firestone wide base rim was

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:45.110
adopted by passenger car manufacturers the next year wasn't it Johnny that's

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:51.370
right Wilbur in 1941 started off with plenty of excitement too we had a fire

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:55.640
in the garage area on the morning of the race quite a few cars were lost it was

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:59.740
bad luck all the way around you grabbed off most of the tough luck in that race

00:13:59.740 --> 00:13:59.960
We'll...

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.790
or more than my share he was way out in front near the end of the race only to

00:14:04.790 --> 00:14:08.700
have a rear hub brake but hadn't been for that he'd probably be the only four

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:19.790
time winner in the history of the Speedway after they picked me off the

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:25.200
wall Maury Rose went on to win driving relief for Floyd Davis I think the most

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:29.720
remarkable thing about the 1941 race the fact that Cliff Borgere around the

00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:29.900
entire

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:35.280
500 miles finished in fifth place and didn't make a single pit stop that was

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:39.520
the first time it ever happened in the history of the speedway anything else on

00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:46.370
1941 how about it boys offhand that's all I can think of and that's the last

00:14:46.370 --> 00:14:51.870
race before the war yep this ought to make pretty good reading my syndicate

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:56.960
wanted a story on the real value of the Indianapolis 500 mile race this

00:14:56.960 --> 00:14:58.140
material ought to do it

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:04.760
Yes, Mr. Reed, the great Indianapolis race has contributed much to this nation.

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:11.350
Ideas born on the speedway helped to produce the modern automobile and many other important devices.

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:22.960
Indianapolis racing experience paid rich dividends when the armed forces insisted that every manufacturer of aircraft tires adopt Firestone Speedway-approved tire construction.

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:29.960
Every type of combat vehicle demanded the strongest possible tires to withstand the terrific strain.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:36.590
of war service and here again the experience gained by Firestone at Indianapolis proved to

00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:43.360
be vitally important in the research laboratories of the rubber industry as in every other developmental

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:48.460
facility of the nation new materials and new processes were rushed to fill the needs of

00:15:48.460 --> 00:15:55.330
the armed forces the entire Firestone organization placed its resources of experience manpower and

00:15:55.330 --> 00:15:59.600
technical equipment behind the government program for the development of synthetic rubber

00:16:00.410 --> 00:16:06.140
carrying out a project which Harvey S. Firestone had urged upon the nation years earlier in

00:16:06.320 --> 00:16:13.820
prospect of just such a dire emergence. From intensive research emerged not only a satisfactory

00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:20.120
substitute for natural rubber, but also synthetics which were definitely superior to natural rubber

00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:26.870
in many uses. And in wartime, the Indianapolis Speedway played a vital role in the development

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:29.770
of better tires. When tire conservation

00:16:30.090 --> 00:16:34.800
was a vital necessity the government had enforced a 35 mile an hour speed limit

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:39.390
throughout the country in order to save rubber but realizing that this control

00:16:39.390 --> 00:16:43.820
must one day be lifted automobile manufacturers as well as the government

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:48.420
were wondering whether the synthetic tires would be safe at faster highway

00:16:48.420 --> 00:16:55.880
speed laboratory tests said yes but the final test is road driving and so the

00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:59.870
government authorized the use of gasoline and tires to find out just how

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:08.230
safe synthetic tires were at higher speed. In 1944, Wilbur Shaw made a

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:13.100
tremendously important run before the empty stands at Indianapolis. Stock

00:17:13.150 --> 00:17:17.430
Firestone Deluxe Champion synthetic tires were placed on the wheels of a

00:17:17.430 --> 00:17:22.570
racing car. Under the supervision of the American Automobile Association, Shaw

00:17:22.570 --> 00:17:24.970
began a 500-mile race against time.

00:17:30.190 --> 00:17:35.450
these standard firestone passenger car tires stood up to the test as the car roared around

00:17:35.450 --> 00:17:42.520
the turns on the rough emery-like surface of the speedway tires were subjected to almost

00:17:42.520 --> 00:17:49.290
unbelievable stresses the terrific centrifugal force on the curves tried to tear the tires from

00:17:49.290 --> 00:17:56.180
the wheel they held firmly grinding against the abrasive surface of track racing speeds

00:17:56.180 --> 00:17:58.060
were maintained on the straightaway

00:17:58.060 --> 00:17:59.970
and on the turns without a...

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:09.860
single tire failure. 500 miles, Wilbur Shaw raced around the great speedway, and he crossed

00:18:09.860 --> 00:18:16.370
the line with an official average speed of better than 100 miles per hour. This was the

00:18:16.370 --> 00:18:22.900
grueling test of the speedway, clearly demonstrating that Firestone synthetic rubber tires are safe

00:18:22.900 --> 00:18:29.970
at high speed. And remember, 500 miles on the speedway are the equal of...

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:32.240
50,000 miles on the highway.

00:18:33.620 --> 00:18:39.030
After four years of war, the great two-and-a-half-mile track again echoes to the sound of roaring

00:18:39.030 --> 00:18:39.250
in.

00:18:40.060 --> 00:18:45.400
The smell of hot oil hangs heavy over the pits of the garage, where drivers and mechanics

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:47.600
put the finishing touches on their shining mounts.

00:18:48.910 --> 00:18:52.190
On the test stand, wheels are aligned and balanced.

00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:55.670
Vital parts are magnaflux to reveal hidden dangers.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:06.150
tires and tubes are carefully inspected, mounted, and inflated under the watchful eye of Firestone

00:19:06.150 --> 00:19:13.220
racing engineers. That tall chap with a mustache is Cliff Berger, who's driven more miles in

00:19:13.220 --> 00:19:20.400
competition at Indianapolis than any other man. Say, Johnny, I think I better have one more 750

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:25.330
20 mounted up, even if I don't expect to use it. Okay, Cliff, we'll mount it up and just charge it

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:29.980
to your account. When drivers like Cliff Berger buy Firestone...

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:35.710
tires to use in the 500-mile race, they know that they are buying the best kind of safety

00:19:35.710 --> 00:19:42.230
insurance. Also important are the critical tests which cars and drivers are required to

00:19:42.230 --> 00:19:47.970
pass before the contest board officials will allow them to compete. Despite the fact that

00:19:47.970 --> 00:19:53.950
this Maserati carried Wilbur Shaw to two Indianapolis victories, it must be weighed, measured, and

